<?xml version="1.0" encoding="utf-8"?>

<section xml:id="sec-continuous-probability-i" xmlns:xi="http://www.w3.org/2001/XInclude">
    <title>Continuous Probability I</title>
    <objectives component="outcomes">
        <introduction>
            <p>
            <dl>
                <li>
                    <title>Strand</title>
                    <p>3.0 Statistics and Probability</p>
                </li>
                <li>
                    <title>Sub-Strand</title>
                    <p>3.2 Probability II</p>
                </li>
                <li>
                    <title>Specific Learning Outcomes</title>
                    <p>Determine discrete and continuous probabilities in different situations</p>
                </li>
                <li component="lesson-plan">
                    <title>Grade Level</title>
                    <p>11</p>
                </li>
                <li component="lesson-plan">
                    <title>Estimated Duration</title>
                    <p>40 minutes</p>
                </li>
            </dl>
            </p>
        </introduction>
    </objectives>

    <axiom component="lesson-plan">
        <title>Problem-Solving and Discovery</title>
        <dl>
            <li>
                <title>Suggested Time</title>
                <p>12 minutes</p>
            </li>
            <li>
                <title>Objective</title>
                <p>
                    Learners discover that for a continuous quantity, probability corresponds to an area rather than a sum of listed values — and that the probability of any single exact value is zero.
                </p>
            </li>
            <li>
                <title>Teacher's Role</title>
                <p>
                    Do not introduce the formula <m>f(x) = \frac{1}{b-a}</m> yet. Let learners reason geometrically: the rectangle must have area 1 (total probability), so they can find its height from base × height = 1. Ask after part (d): <q>Why can't we list P(X = 7), P(X = 7.1), P(X = 7.01), … as we did in the discrete case?</q>
                </p>
            </li>
        </dl>
    </axiom>

    <activity xml:id="act-matatu-waiting-time">
        <title>Waiting for a Matatu</title>
        <statement>
            <p>
                Achieng arrives at a matatu stop at a random time. From past observation, the next matatu could arrive anywhere between 0 and 20 minutes from now, with every moment equally likely. Let <m>X</m> = the waiting time in minutes.
            </p>
            <p>
                Because <m>X</m> is measured (not counted), it can take any value such as 3, 7.4, or 12.85 — it is a <em>continuous</em> random variable.
            </p>
            <p>
                <ol marker="(a)">
                    <li>
                        <p>
                            Draw a rectangle on a set of axes where the horizontal axis shows values of <m>x</m> from 0 to 20, and the vertical axis shows the height of the rectangle. The total area of the rectangle must equal 1. What is the height of the rectangle?
                        </p>
                        <solution component="lesson-plan">
                            <p>
                                Area = base × height = <m>20 \times h = 1</m>, so height <m>h = \frac{1}{20} = 0.05</m>. Learners should label both axes before computing.
                            </p>
                        </solution>
                    </li>
                    <li>
                        <p>
                            Shade the strip of the rectangle where <m>5 \leq x \leq 10</m>. What fraction of the total area does the shaded strip represent? This fraction is <m>P(5 \leq X \leq 10)</m>.
                        </p>
                        <solution component="lesson-plan">
                            <p>
                                Area of strip = <m>(10 - 5) \times \frac{1}{20} = \frac{5}{20} = \frac{1}{4} = 0.25</m>. The shaded strip is one quarter of the total rectangle.
                            </p>
                        </solution>
                    </li>
                    <li>
                        <p>
                            Find <m>P(X \leq 8)</m> by computing the relevant area.
                        </p>
                        <solution component="lesson-plan">
                            <p>
                                <m>P(X \leq 8) = 8 \times \frac{1}{20} = \frac{8}{20} = \frac{2}{5} = 0.4</m>.
                            </p>
                        </solution>
                    </li>
                    <li>
                        <p>
                            What is <m>P(X = 7)</m> — the probability that the matatu arrives in exactly 7 minutes? Explain your reasoning using the rectangle.
                        </p>
                        <solution component="lesson-plan">
                            <p>
                                A single point <m>x = 7</m> has zero width, so the strip at that point has zero area. Therefore <m>P(X = 7) = 0</m>. This is the essential difference from a discrete random variable: for a continuous random variable, the probability of any single exact value is always zero.
                            </p>
                        </solution>
                    </li>
                </ol>
            </p>
        </statement>
    </activity>

    <axiom component="lesson-plan">
        <title>Structured Instruction</title>
        <dl>
            <li>
                <title>Suggested Time</title>
                <p>12 minutes</p>
            </li>
            <li>
                <title>Objective</title>
                <p>
                    To formalise the uniform distribution, the probability-as-area formula, and the cumulative distribution function (CDF).
                </p>
            </li>
            <li>
                <title>Teacher's Role</title>
                <p>
                    Name the distribution: the rectangle they drew is the <term>continuous uniform distribution</term> on <m>[0, 20]</m>, written <m>X \sim U(0, 20)</m>. Generalise: for <m>X \sim U(a, b)</m>, the height is <m>f(x) = \frac{1}{b-a}</m> for <m>a \leq x \leq b</m>, and <m>f(x) = 0</m> outside this range.
                </p>
                <p>
                    Write the probability formula on the board:
                    <md>P(c \leq X \leq d) = \frac{d - c}{b - a}.</md>
                    Emphasise that <m>P(c \leq X \leq d) = P(c &lt; X &lt; d)</m> for continuous distributions — the endpoints make no difference to the area since single points have zero probability.
                </p>
                <p>
                    Introduce the cumulative distribution function: <m>P(X \leq x) = \frac{x - a}{b - a}</m>. Ask students for the probability that the next matatu arrives within 15 minutes or less. Point to the activity as a concrete instance with <m>a = 0</m>, <m>b = 20</m>.
                </p>
            </li>
        </dl>
    </axiom>

    <insight>
        <title>Key Ideas</title>
        <p>
            <ul>
                <li>
                    <p>
                        A <term>continuous random variable</term> <m>X</m> takes any value in an interval — values obtained by measuring, not counting. Examples include waiting time, height, temperature, and rainfall.
                    </p>
                </li>
                <li>
                    <p>
                        For a continuous random variable, the <term>probability density function</term> (PDF) <m>f(x)</m> is a curve or line. <em>Probability equals area</em> under the curve over an interval — not a sum of individual values.
                    </p>
                </li>
                <li>
                    <p>
                        The <term>uniform distribution</term> <m>U(a, b)</m> models a quantity equally likely to fall anywhere in <m>[a, b]</m>. Its PDF is:
                        <md>f(x) = \frac{1}{b - a} \quad \text{for } a \leq x \leq b,</md>
                        and <m>f(x) = 0</m> outside this interval. The rectangle has total area 1.
                    </p>
                </li>
                <li>
                    <p>
                        <term>Probability formula</term>: For <m>X \sim U(a, b)</m> and <m>a \leq c \leq d \leq b</m>,
                        <md>P(c \leq X \leq d) = \frac{d - c}{b - a}.</md>
                    </p>
                </li>
                <li>
                    <p>
                        <term>Key fact</term>: For any continuous random variable, <m>P(X = c) = 0</m> for every specific value <m>c</m>. Therefore <m>P(c \leq X \leq d) = P(c &lt; X &lt; d)</m>.
                    </p>
                </li>
                <li>
                    <p>
                        <term>Cumulative distribution function (CDF)</term>: For <m>X \sim U(a, b)</m>,
                        <md>P(X \leq x) = \frac{x - a}{b - a} \quad \text{for } a \leq x \leq b.</md>
                    </p>
                </li>
            </ul>
        </p>
    </insight>

    <figure xml:id="fig-uniform-pdf">
        <caption>
            The PDF of <m>X \sim U(0, 20)</m>. The full rectangle has height <m>\frac{1}{20}</m> and total area 1. The shaded region shows <m>P(5 \leq X \leq 10) = \frac{5}{20} = \frac{1}{4}</m>.
        </caption>
        <image width="68%">
            <latex-image>
                \begin{tikzpicture}[scale=1.0]
                    % Unshaded parts of rectangle (light blue)
                    \fill[blue!10] (0,0) rectangle (1.5,1.8);
                    \fill[blue!10] (3.0,0) rectangle (6.0,1.8);
                    % Shaded strip: x = 1.5 to 3.0 (representing 5 to 10 minutes)
                    \fill[blue!40] (1.5,0) rectangle (3.0,1.8);
                    % Rectangle outline
                    \draw[blue, thick] (0,0) -- (0,1.8) -- (6.0,1.8) -- (6.0,0) -- (0,0);
                    % Strip boundaries
                    \draw[blue, thick] (1.5,0) -- (1.5,1.8);
                    \draw[blue, thick] (3.0,0) -- (3.0,1.8);
                    % Axes
                    \draw[-&gt;, thick] (-0.4,0) -- (6.9,0) node[right] {$x$ (min)};
                    \draw[-&gt;, thick] (0,-0.4) -- (0,2.5) node[above] {$f(x)$};
                    % x-axis ticks: 0, 5, 10, 15, 20
                    \foreach \pos/\lab in {0/0, 1.5/5, 3.0/10, 4.5/15, 6.0/20}
                        \draw (\pos,0.07) -- (\pos,-0.07) node[below] {\small $\lab$};
                    % y-axis tick and label at 1/20
                    \draw (-0.07,1.8) -- (0.07,1.8);
                    \node[left] at (-0.07,1.8) {\small $\dfrac{1}{20}$};
                    % Dashed guide line from y-axis across top of rectangle
                    \draw[dashed, gray!70] (0,1.8) -- (6.1,1.8);
                    % Label inside shaded strip
                    \node[align=center] at (2.25,0.9) {\small $\dfrac{1}{4}$};
                \end{tikzpicture}
            </latex-image>
        </image>
    </figure>

    <xi:include href="../lesson-plan-templates/example-intro.ptx" />

    <example>
        <title>Basic Uniform Probabilities</title>
        <statement>
            <p>
                Buses on a Nairobi route arrive at a stop at uniformly distributed intervals. The waiting time <m>X</m> (in minutes) satisfies <m>X \sim U(0, 30)</m>.
            </p>
            <p>
                <ol marker="(a)">
                    <li><p>Write down <m>f(x)</m>, the PDF of <m>X</m>.</p></li>
                    <li><p>Find <m>P(10 \leq X \leq 20)</m>.</p></li>
                    <li><p>Find <m>P(X \leq 12)</m>.</p></li>
                    <li><p>Find <m>P(X &gt; 25)</m>.</p></li>
                </ol>
            </p>
        </statement>
        <solution>
            <p>
                <ol marker="(a)">
                    <li>
                        <p>
                            <md>f(x) = \frac{1}{30 - 0} = \frac{1}{30} \quad \text{for } 0 \leq x \leq 30.</md>
                        </p>
                    </li>
                    <li>
                        <p>
                            <md>P(10 \leq X \leq 20) = \frac{20 - 10}{30} = \frac{10}{30} = \frac{1}{3}.</md>
                        </p>
                    </li>
                    <li>
                        <p>
                            <md>P(X \leq 12) = \frac{12 - 0}{30} = \frac{12}{30} = \frac{2}{5}.</md>
                        </p>
                    </li>
                    <li>
                        <p>
                            <md>P(X &gt; 25) = \frac{30 - 25}{30} = \frac{5}{30} = \frac{1}{6}.</md>
                        </p>
                    </li>
                </ol>
            </p>
        </solution>
    </example>

    <example>
        <title>Finding an Unknown Value from a Given Probability</title>
        <statement>
            <p>
                The daily high temperature in Kisumu during July is uniformly distributed between 18°C and 30°C. Let <m>T \sim U(18, 30)</m>.
            </p>
            <p>
                <ol marker="(a)">
                    <li><p>Find <m>P(T &gt; 24)</m>.</p></li>
                    <li><p>Find the temperature <m>t</m> such that <m>P(T \leq t) = 0.25</m>.</p></li>
                </ol>
            </p>
        </statement>
        <solution>
            <p>
                The width of the distribution is <m>b - a = 30 - 18 = 12</m>, so <m>f(t) = \frac{1}{12}</m>.
            </p>
            <p>
                <ol marker="(a)">
                    <li>
                        <p>
                            <md>P(T &gt; 24) = \frac{30 - 24}{12} = \frac{6}{12} = \frac{1}{2}.</md>
                            There is a 50% probability that the daily high exceeds 24°C.
                        </p>
                    </li>
                    <li>
                        <p>
                            Using the CDF formula <m>P(T \leq t) = \frac{t - 18}{12}</m>:
                            <md>\frac{t - 18}{12} = 0.25 \implies t - 18 = 3 \implies t = 21\text{°C}.</md>
                        </p>
                    </li>
                </ol>
            </p>
        </solution>
    </example>

    <example>
        <title>Using the CDF and Complement Rule</title>
        <statement>
            <p>
                A school tap fills a bucket in a time <m>X</m> that is uniformly distributed between 4 and 16 minutes. Let <m>X \sim U(4, 16)</m>.
            </p>
            <p>
                <ol marker="(a)">
                    <li><p>Write down <m>f(x)</m>.</p></li>
                    <li><p>Find <m>P(X \leq 10)</m>.</p></li>
                    <li><p>Find <m>P(X &gt; 13)</m>.</p></li>
                    <li><p>Find <m>P(6 \leq X \leq 14)</m>.</p></li>
                </ol>
            </p>
        </statement>
        <solution>
            <p>
                The width of the distribution is <m>16 - 4 = 12</m>.
            </p>
            <p>
                <ol marker="(a)">
                    <li>
                        <p>
                            <md>f(x) = \frac{1}{12} \quad \text{for } 4 \leq x \leq 16.</md>
                        </p>
                    </li>
                    <li>
                        <p>
                            <md>P(X \leq 10) = \frac{10 - 4}{12} = \frac{6}{12} = \frac{1}{2}.</md>
                        </p>
                    </li>
                    <li>
                        <p>
                            <md>P(X &gt; 13) = \frac{16 - 13}{12} = \frac{3}{12} = \frac{1}{4}.</md>
                        </p>
                    </li>
                    <li>
                        <p>
                            <md>P(6 \leq X \leq 14) = \frac{14 - 6}{12} = \frac{8}{12} = \frac{2}{3}.</md>
                        </p>
                    </li>
                </ol>
            </p>
        </solution>
    </example>

    <example>
        <title>Combining Probability Rules</title>
        <statement>
            <p>
                A school cafeteria in Nairobi serves lunch over a 90-minute period. A student arrives at a uniformly random time during the lunch period. Let <m>X</m> = the number of minutes into the period when the student arrives, so <m>X \sim U(0, 90)</m>.
            </p>
            <p>
                <ol marker="(a)">
                    <li><p>Find <m>P(20 \leq X \leq 50)</m>.</p></li>
                    <li><p>Find <m>P(X &lt; 30 \text{ or } X &gt; 75)</m>.</p></li>
                    <li><p>Find the value <m>m</m> such that <m>P(X \leq m) = \frac{2}{3}</m>.</p></li>
                </ol>
            </p>
        </statement>
        <solution>
            <p>
                Here <m>f(x) = \frac{1}{90}</m> for <m>0 \leq x \leq 90</m>.
            </p>
            <p>
                <ol marker="(a)">
                    <li>
                        <p>
                            <md>P(20 \leq X \leq 50) = \frac{50 - 20}{90} = \frac{30}{90} = \frac{1}{3}.</md>
                        </p>
                    </li>
                    <li>
                        <p>
                            The events <m>\{X &lt; 30\}</m> and <m>\{X &gt; 75\}</m> do not overlap, so:
                            <md>P(X &lt; 30) + P(X &gt; 75) = \frac{30}{90} + \frac{90 - 75}{90} = \frac{30}{90} + \frac{15}{90} = \frac{45}{90} = \frac{1}{2}.</md>
                        </p>
                    </li>
                    <li>
                        <p>
                            Using the CDF:
                            <md>\frac{m}{90} = \frac{2}{3} \implies m = 90 \times \frac{2}{3} = 60 \text{ minutes}.</md>
                        </p>
                    </li>
                </ol>
            </p>
        </solution>
    </example>

    <xi:include href="../lesson-plan-templates/assessment-intro.ptx" />

    <exercises>
        <exercise>
            <statement>
                <p>
                    Let <m>X \sim U(0, 8)</m>.
                </p>
                <p>
                    <ol marker="(a)">
                        <li><p>Write down <m>f(x)</m>.</p></li>
                        <li><p>Find <m>P(2 \leq X \leq 6)</m>.</p></li>
                        <li><p>Find <m>P(X &gt; 5)</m>.</p></li>
                    </ol>
                </p>
            </statement>
            <answer>
                <p>
                    <ol marker="(a)">
                        <li>
                            <p>
                                <m>f(x) = \frac{1}{8}</m> for <m>0 \leq x \leq 8</m>.
                            </p>
                        </li>
                        <li>
                            <p>
                                <m>P(2 \leq X \leq 6) = \frac{6-2}{8} = \frac{4}{8} = \frac{1}{2}</m>.
                            </p>
                        </li>
                        <li>
                            <p>
                                <m>P(X &gt; 5) = \frac{8-5}{8} = \frac{3}{8}</m>.
                            </p>
                        </li>
                    </ol>
                </p>
            </answer>
        </exercise>

        <exercise>
            <statement>
                <p>
                    A delivery van arrives at a depot at a uniformly distributed time between 6:00 am and 10:00 am. Find the probability that it arrives between 7:30 am and 9:00 am.
                </p>
            </statement>
            <answer>
                <p>
                    The total window is 240 minutes. The interval 7:30–9:00 am is 90 minutes long.
                    <m>P = \frac{90}{240} = \frac{3}{8}</m>.
                </p>
            </answer>
        </exercise>

        <exercise>
            <statement>
                <p>
                    Daily rainfall in Kisumu during April is uniformly distributed between 5 mm and 25 mm. Let <m>X \sim U(5, 25)</m>. Find:
                </p>
                <p>
                    <ol marker="(a)">
                        <li><p><m>P(X \leq 15)</m></p></li>
                        <li><p><m>P(X &gt; 22)</m></p></li>
                        <li><p><m>P(10 \leq X \leq 20)</m></p></li>
                    </ol>
                </p>
            </statement>
            <answer>
                <p>
                    The width of the distribution is <m>25 - 5 = 20</m>.
                </p>
                <p>
                    <ol marker="(a)">
                        <li>
                            <p>
                                <m>P(X \leq 15) = \frac{15-5}{20} = \frac{10}{20} = \frac{1}{2}</m>.
                            </p>
                        </li>
                        <li>
                            <p>
                                <m>P(X &gt; 22) = \frac{25-22}{20} = \frac{3}{20}</m>.
                            </p>
                        </li>
                        <li>
                            <p>
                                <m>P(10 \leq X \leq 20) = \frac{20-10}{20} = \frac{10}{20} = \frac{1}{2}</m>.
                            </p>
                        </li>
                    </ol>
                </p>
            </answer>
        </exercise>

        <exercise>
            <statement>
                <p>
                    Let <m>X \sim U(4, 16)</m>. Find the value <m>q</m> such that <m>P(X &gt; q) = \frac{1}{4}</m>.
                </p>
            </statement>
            <answer>
                <p>
                    <m>P(X &gt; q) = \frac{16 - q}{12} = \frac{1}{4}</m>, so <m>16 - q = 3</m>, giving <m>q = 13</m>.
                </p>
            </answer>
        </exercise>

        <exercise>
            <statement>
                <p>
                    A continuous random variable <m>X</m> is uniformly distributed on the interval <m>[0, b]</m> for some unknown value <m>b &gt; 0</m>. Given that <m>P(X &lt; 6) = 0.4</m>, find <m>b</m>.
                </p>
            </statement>
            <answer>
                <p>
                    Using the CDF: <m>\frac{6}{b} = 0.4</m>, so <m>b = \frac{6}{0.4} = 15</m>.
                </p>
            </answer>
        </exercise>
    </exercises>

